1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,600
Change Alley: Singapore's Lane
of Money-Changers and Bargains

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,693
For about a century, the
quickest way from the

3
00:00:07,773 --> 00:00:11,072
sea to the heart of
Singapore's business district

4
00:00:11,152 --> 00:00:14,325
was a narrow lane about
a hundred metres long.

5
00:00:14,325 --> 00:00:16,578
Change Alley ran from Collyer Quay,

6
00:00:16,658 --> 00:00:20,445
where sailors and passengers
came ashore at Clifford Pier,

7
00:00:20,525 --> 00:00:24,325
to Raffles Place, the square
of banks and trading houses.

8
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:27,961
It was named, by most accounts,
for the money-changers who

9
00:00:28,041 --> 00:00:31,549
did business along it, and
over the years it also became

10
00:00:31,629 --> 00:00:35,073
one of the busiest bazaars
in the city, where tourists,

11
00:00:35,153 --> 00:00:39,046
sailors and the clerks and office
workers from the surrounding

12
00:00:39,126 --> 00:00:42,650
buildings haggled over watches,
pens, shirts and cloth.

13
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:46,891
The open-air alley closed
on the 30th of April 1989,

14
00:00:46,971 --> 00:00:51,375
and an air-conditioned arcade
now stands on the site.

15
00:00:51,375 --> 00:00:55,784
The lane got its official name
on the 11th of November 1890,

16
00:00:55,864 --> 00:00:58,777
when Singapore's municipal commissioners

17
00:00:58,857 --> 00:01:01,850
chose "Change Alley" over "Change Lane".

18
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:04,950
There are two explanations for the name.

19
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:09,951
One links it to Exchange Alley in
London, also known as Change Alley,

20
00:01:10,031 --> 00:01:13,050
a historic centre of trading in the City.

21
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:16,807
The other, and the one most
often repeated in Singapore,

22
00:01:16,887 --> 00:01:20,175
links it to the money-changers
who worked there.

23
00:01:20,175 --> 00:01:24,000
Whatever its origin, the
alley was a short cut.

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,224
At the Collyer Quay end
stood Winchester House,

25
00:01:27,304 --> 00:01:31,583
a four-storey building dating
from 1905, and the lane ran from

26
00:01:31,663 --> 00:01:35,600
there between the commercial
buildings to Raffles Place.

27
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:38,061
For anyone landing at the waterfront,

28
00:01:38,141 --> 00:01:41,850
it was the most direct way
into the business district.

29
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:45,454
The money-changers of Change
Alley were mostly Indian,

30
00:01:45,534 --> 00:01:50,650
and they ran their businesses from
small shops of their own along the lane.

31
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:55,200
They appear in the newspapers
from at least the 1920s.

32
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,963
In October 1929, the
Singapore Free Press reported

33
00:01:59,043 --> 00:02:02,575
that burglars had broken
into "two Indian money

34
00:02:02,655 --> 00:02:06,575
changers' shops in Change
Alley" in a single night.

35
00:02:06,575 --> 00:02:10,059
For a port city full of
ships' crews and passengers

36
00:02:10,139 --> 00:02:14,111
carrying the money of a dozen
countries, a row of changers

37
00:02:14,191 --> 00:02:17,825
a few steps from the landing
pier was a convenience.

38
00:02:17,825 --> 00:02:21,050
Not every changer worked from a shop.

39
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:24,799
In later years, unlicensed
money-changers stood at both

40
00:02:24,879 --> 00:02:28,418
entrances to the alley,
offering foreign currency at

41
00:02:28,498 --> 00:02:32,456
rates that could be negotiated,
and one visitor remembered

42
00:02:32,536 --> 00:02:36,225
them flashing foreign
banknotes to attract customers.

43
00:02:36,225 --> 00:02:40,486
In 1932 The Malayan Saturday
Post called the alley "one of the

44
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:45,248
busiest spots of Singapore", with
bargain counters that drew "crowds

45
00:02:45,328 --> 00:02:49,379
of all classes throughout the
day", and noted how quiet and

46
00:02:49,459 --> 00:02:54,150
almost eerie the same passage became
once business hours were over.

47
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:58,761
In February 1931 a writer for
the Singapore Free Press walked

48
00:02:58,841 --> 00:03:03,225
into the alley at about noon
and described what he found.

49
00:03:03,225 --> 00:03:06,300
He compared it with two London streets.

50
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:09,965
In earlier years it had been
Singapore's Mincing Lane,

51
00:03:10,045 --> 00:03:13,850
the street of London's commodity
brokers, and now it was

52
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:17,675
its Petticoat Lane, London's
best-known street market.

53
00:03:17,675 --> 00:03:21,650
At the back of the alley was
an exchange room where Chinese

54
00:03:21,730 --> 00:03:25,979
produce dealers sat at a long
table reading Chinese newspapers,

55
00:03:26,059 --> 00:03:28,453
trading gambier, pepper, copra, tin,

56
00:03:28,533 --> 00:03:33,000
tapioca and sago, and slipping
into back rooms to sign contracts.

57
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,695
Compradors for the European firms and

58
00:03:35,775 --> 00:03:38,400
commission agents worked there too.

59
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,600
According to the writer, that
side of the alley was fading.

60
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:46,024
Ten years earlier, Change
Alley had been a name known

61
00:03:46,104 --> 00:03:48,866
in business circles in
London and New York,

62
00:03:48,946 --> 00:03:53,375
through the commission agents who
bought and sold raw rubber there.

63
00:03:53,375 --> 00:03:58,270
By 1931 most of that business
was done by telephone in offices,

64
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:01,271
and the alley's importance in business

65
00:04:01,351 --> 00:04:05,300
life had become, in his
words, only "second rate".

66
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:08,375
In its place had come the bazaar.

67
00:04:08,375 --> 00:04:13,001
Hawkers called out their prices
in Malay, ten cents, twenty cents,

68
00:04:13,081 --> 00:04:17,707
five cents, and sold fountain pens
from Germany, silks from Japan,

69
00:04:17,787 --> 00:04:22,350
iced water, toffee, fruit, shirts,
scissors, pencils and curios.

70
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:26,720
Retailers had set up shops by
the dozen, alongside barbers,

71
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:31,250
eating places and stairways
leading up to lawyers' offices.

72
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:34,490
The writer thought there
could be no more business

73
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:37,425
per square foot anywhere
else in Singapore.

74
00:04:37,425 --> 00:04:39,874
He also noted, with some amusement,

75
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:44,282
the municipal notice at the
Raffles Place entrance announcing

76
00:04:44,362 --> 00:04:47,975
that no hawkers were allowed
to ply in the street.

77
00:04:47,975 --> 00:04:53,150
By the end of the 1930s the
bazaar was the main attraction.

78
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:57,001
In February 1939 The Sunday
Times gave its back page

79
00:04:57,081 --> 00:05:01,008
to photographs of the alley,
describing it as a place

80
00:05:01,088 --> 00:05:04,561
that sold "anything from
a stud to a lampshade,

81
00:05:04,641 --> 00:05:08,950
from tinned goods to cosmetics"
in its 50 yards of shops.

82
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:12,227
Prices were seldom fixed,
and shoppers enjoyed

83
00:05:12,307 --> 00:05:15,300
cutting a merchant's first price by half.

84
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:19,560
Where beggars had once sat at
the entrances, newspaper boys now

85
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:24,600
competed to sell the latest editions,
and a bootblack worked at one end.

86
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,677
The paper suggested that the
alley's appeal was that shoppers

87
00:05:28,757 --> 00:05:33,050
could inspect the goods closely
without feeling obliged to buy.

88
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:37,800
After the Second World War, the
goods became even more varied.

89
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:42,037
There were clothes, briefcases,
watches, toys, fishing tackle,

90
00:05:42,117 --> 00:05:47,200
handicrafts and souvenirs, as well as
tailors, shoe-shiners and cobblers.

91
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:50,712
Small shops shared the
lane with makeshift tables,

92
00:05:50,792 --> 00:05:55,175
and roving salesmen carried
wooden boxes of pens and watches.

93
00:05:55,175 --> 00:05:58,276
A covering of zinc,
plastic and canvas sheets

94
00:05:58,356 --> 00:06:01,750
kept off most of the rain,
though not all of it.

95
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:05,475
Two groups of customers
kept the bazaar going.

96
00:06:05,475 --> 00:06:08,000
The first came from the sea.

97
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,633
Tourists from passenger ships,
sailors on shore leave and

98
00:06:11,713 --> 00:06:15,542
servicemen landed at Clifford
Pier, across the road from the

99
00:06:15,622 --> 00:06:19,400
alley's seaward entrance, and
walked through it into town.

100
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:22,325
To serve them, some shopkeepers picked up

101
00:06:22,405 --> 00:06:25,850
phrases in French, German,
Italian and Russian.

102
00:06:25,850 --> 00:06:29,425
The second group worked
a few minutes' walk away.

103
00:06:29,425 --> 00:06:33,109
As more Asian clerks and staff
joined the offices around

104
00:06:33,189 --> 00:06:36,134
Raffles Place and Collyer
Quay after the war,

105
00:06:36,214 --> 00:06:40,168
office workers came to the alley
for watches, fountain pens,

106
00:06:40,248 --> 00:06:44,550
handbags and cheap textiles that
could be made into office wear.

107
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:48,243
In a crowded, narrow lane
with no fixed prices,

108
00:06:48,323 --> 00:06:50,250
bargaining was expected.

109
00:06:50,250 --> 00:06:53,828
One shopper, recalling it
decades later, put it simply:

110
00:06:53,908 --> 00:06:57,500
"In Change Alley, if you
don't bargain you're a fool."

111
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,476
In 1973, after a revamp of Clifford Pier,

112
00:07:00,556 --> 00:07:05,321
an air-conditioned shopping arcade
called the Change Alley Aerial

113
00:07:05,401 --> 00:07:09,650
Plaza opened on the bridge
linking the pier to the alley.

114
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:13,925
The old lane itself, however,
was losing its customers.

115
00:07:13,925 --> 00:07:17,333
Through the 1980s, fewer
visitors arrived by sea,

116
00:07:17,413 --> 00:07:20,750
the foreign forces had
withdrawn from Singapore,

117
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:25,600
and shoppers had moved to the new
air-conditioned shopping centres.

118
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:30,625
The alley also sat on some of the
most valuable land in the city.

119
00:07:30,625 --> 00:07:35,746
In February 1989 The Straits Times
reported that the alley's last 55

120
00:07:35,826 --> 00:07:40,875
stallholders would have to leave,
to make way for new development.

121
00:07:40,875 --> 00:07:44,682
After weeks of clearance
sales, the shops opened

122
00:07:44,762 --> 00:07:48,325
for the last time on
the 30th of April 1989.

123
00:07:48,325 --> 00:07:52,214
The stallholders were offered
the chance to rent sundry

124
00:07:52,294 --> 00:07:55,975
and cooked-food stalls at
markets and food centres.

125
00:07:55,975 --> 00:08:01,050
Winchester House at the Collyer
Quay end and Singapore Rubber House,

126
00:08:01,130 --> 00:08:04,083
as Shell House had been renamed in 1976,

127
00:08:04,163 --> 00:08:07,650
at the Raffles Place end
were then demolished.

128
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:12,877
I worked at Security Pacific
National Bank in Singapore from 1987,

129
00:08:12,957 --> 00:08:16,575
and I walked through
Change Alley many times.

130
00:08:16,575 --> 00:08:20,100
I don't recall anything
memorable about it.

131
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:24,410
It was where we went to buy snacks
from a Delifrance, and I used the

132
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:28,750
money-changer there too, which was
convenient but not exceptional.

133
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:32,854
Change Alley came back in
1993, when Caltex House (now

134
00:08:32,934 --> 00:08:37,038
Chevron House) and Hitachi
Tower (now 16 Collyer Quay)

135
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:41,225
were completed on the sites
of the two old buildings.

136
00:08:41,225 --> 00:08:44,626
This time it was an
air-conditioned shopping arcade

137
00:08:44,706 --> 00:08:48,244
between the two towers,
joined to the overhead bridge

138
00:08:48,324 --> 00:08:51,600
across Collyer Quay that
is now called OUE Link.

139
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:54,358
Its customers are once again mostly office

140
00:08:54,438 --> 00:08:58,412
workers from the business
district, but the open-air stalls,

141
00:08:58,492 --> 00:09:00,925
the touts and the haggling are gone.

142
00:09:00,925 --> 00:09:05,550
Money-changing itself is now a
licensed business in Singapore,

143
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:08,206
regulated by the Monetary Authority

144
00:09:08,286 --> 00:09:11,625
of Singapore under the
Payment Services Act.

145
00:09:11,625 --> 00:09:16,101
Why it matters today: Change Alley
was never grand, but for a century

146
00:09:16,181 --> 00:09:19,799
it was where Singapore's small
change met its big money,

147
00:09:19,879 --> 00:09:24,619
a hundred metres of money-changers,
hawkers and haggling squeezed between

148
00:09:24,699 --> 00:09:28,925
the waterfront and the banks, and
its name still marks the spot.
